        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Calpains are a group of calcium activated neutral proteases found in all eukaryotic cells. Calpains target many protein constituents of the sarcomere, and compelling evidence indicates that calpain-mediated proteolysis is an early step in the turnover of myofibrils. Calpastatin is a protein that binds specifically to calpains and inhibits their function, and studies have shown that calpain activity is directly modulated by changes in calpastatin protein levels. Reducing calpain activity by increasing calpastatin levels may therefore provide an effective means for efficiently reducing or preventing muscle atrophy. 
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
Experiments will investigate the outcome of forced over expression of calpastatin in vivo. Calpastatin cDNAs driven by muscle-specific inducible promoters will be used to generate transgenic mice. Experiments are using an optimized tet on system to generate transgenic mice in which expression of calpastatin specifically in skeletal muscle cells is controlled by administration of Doxycyline. Hindlimb weighted and unweighted mice over expressing calpastatin are being subjected to detailed analyses for changes in overall muscle size, fiber diameter, nucleus/cytoplasmic ratio and protein turnover rates. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
NASA does not currently have an agreement with NSBRI to archive their data in the LSDA.
        </div>

